From 99f119ffe01e7b3230ec78bca781e3e238eaf54e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: memra-lane <lane@localhost>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 08:14:42 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] dsv4 dense: a no-model family bench that runs BOTH real
 kernels in one process

The served regression survived the setup cache unchanged (87.7-88.0 against
138.6-140.5 tok/s, four arms), and it costs about 4 seconds per 981-token
prefill, which is more than the entire dense family costs the scalar program.
So the question is no longer how much the kernel gained; it is whether the
regression reproduces outside the engine at all.

This bench answers that without a replica. It calls memra_dsv4_gemv_fp8_m, the
engine's own entry point, and selects the arm with arm_dense_cutlass_for_gate,
so both arms are shipped code over the same buffers in one process with no
model load.

The condition under test is WEIGHT RESIDENCY, the one thing the standalone
probe did not model: it reused one weight buffer per shape, so weights sat in
the card's 128 MiB L2 for the whole replay, while the engine cycles about
3.4 GB of distinct layer weights and the CUTLASS path reads a bf16 mirror at
2 bytes per weight where the scalar path reads e4m3 codes at 1.

Each outcome kills something, stated before the run: winning hot and cold moves
the search out of the family and into the engine's surroundings; winning hot
and losing cold makes residency the cause and the mirror's 2x weight bytes the
mechanism; losing both means the 5.25x was an artefact of the earlier harness.

The bench refuses unless the ON arm's split-K count rises and the OFF arm's
does not, and unless the binary contains the path at all. An arm switch that
does nothing reports a clean null, which is exactly what this lane's first
served A/B did with two byte-identical binaries.

Signed-off-by: memra-lane <lane@localhost>
