From f11e24936167ee91bffdb600706a382e84d1c237 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: memra-lane <lane@localhost>
Date: Fri, 11 Sep 2026 14:18:11 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] dsv4 dense: key the split-K workspace and setup cache by
 DEVICE, and count the crossings

The split-K partial workspace was one thread_local pointer. dsv4f serves PP-2
and drives both stages from ONE host thread that binds each stage's context in
turn, so both cards shared one allocation: whichever device was current when it
grew owns the memory, and every call on the other card hands CUTLASS a peer
pointer. The partial is written once and read once per call and is exactly n*k
bytes, so that is 2 n*k bytes crossing PCIe instead of staying on the card, on
roughly half the family's calls. Nothing fails and the answers stay right,
which is the worst shape a defect can take.

The setup cache is keyed by device for the same reason: initialize() queries the
device it runs on, and a params block built against one card should not be
launched on the other.

This is a CANDIDATE for the served 37%, not yet a cause, so it lands with the
